    {
      "id": "equality",
      "title": "deepEqual — against fast-deep-equal",
      "description": "\nStructural equality on raw data (the pure walk, fast-deep-equal's arena) and on canonical data (valsem's O(1)\nshort-circuit, which fast-deep-equal must still walk — over frozen objects, as its users would meet valsem values).\nEvery pair asserts that both libraries return the same verdict; the corpus avoids the two inputs on which their\nsemantics differ (`NaN` and undefined-valued keys). Rows:\n\n- **raw =**: two independently built equal structures — the full walk.\n- **raw ≠**: a difference planted in the middle.\n- **canonical ≠**: two distinct canonical values — valsem answers from the canonicality probe without looking inside.\n- **boundary**: raw wrappers around a shared canonical 100-item subtree — valsem terminates at the boundary.\n",
      "columns": [
        "valsem",
        "fast-deep-equal"
      ],
      "unit": "ns",
      "ratio": [
        "valsem",
        "fast-deep-equal"
      ],
