    {
      "id": "record-copy",
      "title": "Copying a wide record — dictionary mode and megamorphic spread",
      "description": "\nTwo engine facts that decide how fast a record copies, which set how `intern` builds canonical records and how\n`produce` copies a draft. (1) An object grown by assigning keys one at a time into `{}` leaves fast-properties\nmode at ~20 keys on V8; `Object.fromEntries` stays fast at any width — the first rows probe the mode where the\nruntime exposes it (`--allow-natives-syntax`; otherwise they are omitted). (2) Object spread is an inline cache: a\nmemcpy while the site has seen ≤4 shapes, a per-property generic fallback after, and a library's single copy site\nsees every shape in an application; `Object.assign` keys on the source map and stays fast at a megamorphic site.\nCopy rows are for frozen fast-mode sources of 3, 20, 100 and 1,000 keys.\n",
      "columns": [
        "spread, ≤4 shapes",
        "spread, megamorphic",
        "Object.assign, megamorphic"
